/* Bassiet book, Depth 4: The Lymph Garrison. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c4-innate']={depth:4,title:'The Outer Watch',
epi:'The first guards do not ask names. They know the shape of an enemy and strike at once.',
c:['complement','sepsis'],
S:[
{h:'Born knowing',p:[
'Innate immunity is present from birth, fast (minutes to hours), the same on every exposure, and recognises broad molecular patterns rather than specific antigens. Its parts: **barriers** (skin, mucus, cilia, stomach acid, lysozyme, defensins), **phagocytes** (neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells), **natural killer cells**, **complement**, and acute-phase proteins. Neutrophils arrive within hours and die in the fight; macrophages follow, clean up and keep the fire going; dendritic cells carry what they have caught to the lymph node and start the adaptive response.',
'Receptors called pattern recognition receptors read **PAMPs** (pathogen-associated molecular patterns) and **DAMPs** (damage-associated, such as urate crystals and ATP from dead cells). Toll-like receptors sit on the surface or in endosomes; NOD-like receptors and the **inflammasome** sit in the cytoplasm and activate caspase-1, which cuts pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into their active forms. Most Toll-like receptors signal through MyD88 to NF-κB, switching on IL-1, IL-6 and TNF; those that read nucleic acids also switch on type I interferons.'],
table:{cols:['Receptor','Where','Recognises'],rows:[
['TLR2 (with TLR1 or TLR6)','cell surface','lipoteichoic acid and lipoproteins of Gram-positive bacteria; fungal zymosan'],
['TLR4 (with CD14 and MD-2)','cell surface','lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) of Gram-negative bacteria'],
['TLR5','cell surface','flagellin'],
['TLR3','endosome','double-stranded viral RNA'],
['TLR7 and TLR8','endosome','single-stranded viral RNA'],
['TLR9','endosome','unmethylated CpG DNA of bacteria and viruses'],
['NOD2','cytoplasm','muramyl dipeptide from peptidoglycan; loss-of-function variants raise the risk of Crohn disease'],
['NLRP3 inflammasome','cytoplasm','urate crystals, ATP, toxins → caspase-1 → IL-1β; gain-of-function causes cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes']]},
s:[['path','Overactive inflammasomes cause periodic fever syndromes. Familial Mediterranean fever (MEFV, pyrin): episodes of fever with serositis, autosomal recessive, common in Mediterranean and Middle Eastern families; colchicine prevents attacks and AA amyloidosis.'],['pharm','When colchicine fails or the fever syndrome is inflammasome-driven, IL-1 is blocked directly: anakinra (IL-1 receptor antagonist) or canakinumab (antibody against IL-1β).']]},
{h:'Complement',p:[
'Complement is a cascade of plasma proteins. Three pathways all converge on **C3 convertase**: the **classical** pathway (C1q binding antibody in immune complexes; only IgM and IgG can bind C1q), the **alternative** pathway (spontaneous C3 activation on microbial surfaces), and the **lectin** pathway (mannose-binding lectin on microbial sugars).',
'C3 is cut into **C3a** (anaphylatoxin) and **C3b** (opsonin, and part of C5 convertase). C5 is cut into **C5a** (anaphylatoxin and neutrophil chemotaxis) and **C5b**, which starts the **membrane attack complex (C5b–C9)** that punches holes in cells, especially Neisseria.',
'Because C3 activates by itself all the time, the body\'s own cells must keep switching it off. **C1 inhibitor** stops the start of the classical and lectin pathways. On cell membranes, **decay-accelerating factor (CD55)** breaks up convertases and **CD59** blocks assembly of the attack complex. In plasma, **factor H** and **factor I** destroy C3b on host surfaces. Losing a regulator lets complement attack the host.'],
table:{cols:['Deficiency','Result'],rows:[
['C1 esterase inhibitor','hereditary angioedema: bradykinin excess, no urticaria, low C4; ACE inhibitors contraindicated'],
['C1q, C2, C4 (early classical)','lupus-like disease (immune complexes and apoptotic debris not cleared); C2 is the commonest'],
['C3','severe recurrent pyogenic infections, immune complex glomerulonephritis'],
['C5–C9 (MAC)','recurrent Neisseria infections'],
['Factor H or factor I','uncontrolled alternative pathway on endothelium: atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome, C3 glomerulopathy; low C3'],
['CD55 / CD59 (DAF, MIRL)','paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria: complement lyses red cells']]},
key:'Opsonins: C3b and IgG. Anaphylatoxins: C3a, C4a, C5a. Neutrophil chemotaxis: C5a. Lysis: C5b–C9.',
s:[['pharm','Eculizumab and ravulizumab block C5: used in paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria and atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome. Patients must be vaccinated against meningococcus first. Icatibant (bradykinin B2 blocker) and C1 inhibitor concentrate treat hereditary angioedema attacks.'],['micro','The spleen clears encapsulated bacteria opsonised by complement and IgG: after splenectomy, vaccinate against pneumococcus, meningococcus and Haemophilus influenzae type b.']]},
{h:'Natural killers',p:[
'NK cells kill virus-infected and tumour cells that have lost **MHC class I** (a trick viruses and tumours use to hide from CD8 T cells): NK cells kill when the "missing self" signal and activating signals outweigh inhibitory ones. They kill with **perforin and granzymes** and through antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (CD16 binding IgG Fc). They are enhanced by IL-2, IL-12, IFN-α and IFN-β. Marker: **CD16 and CD56**. NK cells need no prior exposure, which makes them innate, though they are lymphocytes.'],
s:[['bio','Interferon α and β, made by virus-infected cells, switch on enzymes in neighbouring cells that degrade viral RNA and stop protein synthesis (an antiviral state), and raise MHC I expression.']]},
{h:'Fever, cytokines and sepsis',p:[
'**IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-α** are the endogenous pyrogens. They make the hypothalamus produce prostaglandin E₂ (through COX-2), which raises the temperature set point; NSAIDs and paracetamol bring fever down by blocking that prostaglandin. The same cytokines drive the liver\'s **acute-phase response**: C-reactive protein (an opsonin that also starts the classical pathway), serum amyloid A, fibrinogen (which raises the ESR), ferritin and hepcidin (which traps iron and causes the anaemia of chronic disease) all rise, while albumin and transferrin fall.',
'When infection triggers an uncontrolled response, the result is **sepsis**: life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response. Nitric oxide dilates vessels, the endothelium leaks, and tissue factor on monocytes and endothelium sets off **disseminated intravascular coagulation**, which uses up platelets and clotting factors. **Septic shock** is sepsis needing vasopressors to keep mean arterial pressure at 65 mmHg or more, with lactate above 2 mmol/L despite fluids.'],
s:[['phys','Septic shock is warm, distributive shock: low systemic vascular resistance, high cardiac output, low wedge pressure, high mixed venous saturation early on. Hypovolaemic and cardiogenic shock have high resistance.'],['pharm','Noradrenaline is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock, after about 30 mL/kg of crystalloid. Antibiotics within the first hour.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A young man has had two episodes of meningococcal meningitis. Which deficiency?',o:['C1 esterase inhibitor','C3','CD55','C5–C9'],a:3,x:'The membrane attack complex is the main defence against Neisseria, so terminal complement deficiency gives repeated meningococcal disease. C3 deficiency also raises infection risk, but with a much broader range of pyogenic bacteria from early childhood.',d:'micro',c:'complement'},
{q:'Recurrent facial and laryngeal swelling without hives; C4 is low between attacks. Which drug must be avoided?',o:['Amlodipine','Lisinopril','Metformin','Atorvastatin'],a:1,x:'Hereditary angioedema is C1 inhibitor deficiency with excess bradykinin, and ACE inhibitors stop bradykinin breakdown, making attacks worse. Amlodipine can cause ankle oedema but does not act on bradykinin.',d:'pharm',c:'complement'},
{q:'Which complement fragment is the main opsonin?',o:['C3b','C3a','C5a','C9'],a:0,x:'C3b coats microbes so phagocytes with complement receptors can grab them; IgG is the other main opsonin. C5a is tempting because it draws neutrophils, but it is a chemoattractant and anaphylatoxin, not an opsonin.',d:'path',c:'complement'},
{q:'A tumour cell stops expressing MHC class I. Which cell is best placed to kill it?',o:['CD8 T cell','CD4 T cell','NK cell','B cell'],a:2,x:'NK cells are restrained by MHC class I, so a cell that loses it is killed ("missing self"). CD8 T cells need MHC class I to see their target, so the loss hides the tumour from them.',d:'path',c:'mhc-tcells'},
{q:'Before eculizumab is started, which vaccine must be given?',o:['Influenza','Hepatitis A','Meningococcal','BCG'],a:2,x:'Blocking C5 removes the membrane attack complex, the same state as a terminal complement deficiency, so Neisseria infections become a real danger. BCG is a live vaccine with no role here.',d:'pharm',c:'complement'},
{q:'In Gram-negative sepsis, which receptor on macrophages recognises lipopolysaccharide?',o:['TLR4 with CD14','TLR3','TLR5','TLR9'],a:0,x:'Lipopolysaccharide is handed by CD14 and MD-2 to TLR4, which signals through NF-κB to release IL-1, IL-6 and TNF. TLR5 recognises flagellin, which Gram-negative rods may also carry, but it does not bind endotoxin.',d:'micro',c:'sepsis'}
]};

BOOK['c4-adaptive']={depth:4,title:'The Inner Keep',
epi:'Inside the keep each guard knows one face. When that face appears, the guard multiplies into an army.',
c:['hiv','hypersensitivity','mhc-tcells','antibodies'],
S:[
{h:'Showing the papers: MHC',p:[
'T cells only recognise antigen as a peptide displayed on **MHC** (major histocompatibility complex; HLA in humans). **MHC class I** (HLA-A, B, C) is on every nucleated cell, presents **endogenous** peptides (viral, tumour) made in the cytosol and loaded in the endoplasmic reticulum through TAP, and is read by **CD8** cytotoxic T cells. **MHC class II** (HLA-DP, DQ, DR) is on antigen-presenting cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells), presents **exogenous** peptides taken up and broken down in endolysosomes (the invariant chain blocks the groove until then), and is read by **CD4** helper T cells. β₂-microglobulin is part of class I.'],
table:{cols:['HLA','Associated disease'],rows:[['B27','ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, IBD-associated arthritis'],['DR2 (DR15)','multiple sclerosis, Goodpasture syndrome, SLE'],['DR3','type 1 diabetes, Graves, SLE, coeliac'],['DR4','rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes'],['DQ2 / DQ8','coeliac disease'],['A3','hereditary haemochromatosis (the HFE gene lies close by)'],['B51','Behçet disease'],['B57:01','abacavir hypersensitivity'],['B58:01','allopurinol severe skin reactions'],['B15:02','carbamazepine Stevens-Johnson syndrome (Southeast Asian ancestry)']]},
s:[['pharm','Test HLA-B*57:01 before abacavir. Test HLA-B*15:02 before carbamazepine in patients of Asian ancestry.']]},
{h:'Training in the thymus',p:[
'T cells mature in the **thymus**. In the cortex, **positive selection** keeps cells whose receptors can bind self-MHC. In the medulla, **negative selection** removes cells that bind self-antigens too strongly; the **AIRE** gene lets medullary cells display proteins from all over the body. AIRE loss causes autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal failure).',
'B cells mature in the bone marrow and are tested for self-reactivity there; survivors go to lymph node follicles. Naive lymphocytes need **two signals** to activate: antigen on MHC plus a co-stimulator (**B7 (CD80/86) on the APC binding CD28** on the T cell). Without the second signal the T cell becomes unresponsive (anergy). CTLA-4 and PD-1 are brakes.'],
s:[['pharm','Checkpoint inhibitors release the brakes: ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4), nivolumab and pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1), atezolizumab (anti-PD-L1). Side effects are autoimmune: colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, hypophysitis, pneumonitis. Abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) blocks co-stimulation in rheumatoid arthritis.']]},
{h:'Helper subsets',p:[
'CD4 helpers specialise according to the cytokines around them when they first meet antigen. Each subset then makes cytokines that strengthen itself and hold back the others, so a response tends to lock into one type.'],
table:{cols:['Subset','Induced by','Secretes','Job'],rows:[
['Th1','IL-12, IFN-γ','IFN-γ, IL-2','activates macrophages and CD8 cells; intracellular microbes; granulomas'],
['Th2','IL-4','IL-4, IL-5, IL-13','IgE class switch, eosinophils; helminths, allergy'],
['Th17','TGF-β, IL-6, IL-23','IL-17, IL-22','neutrophil recruitment; extracellular bacteria and fungi; autoimmunity'],
['Treg','TGF-β, IL-2','IL-10, TGF-β','suppression; FOXP3, CD25'],
['Tfh','IL-6, IL-21','IL-21','help B cells in germinal centres']]},
key:'IFN-γ blocks Th2; IL-4 and IL-10 block Th1. FOXP3 loss → IPEX syndrome (enteropathy, endocrinopathy, dermatitis).',
s:[['micro','Hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome: STAT3 mutation, no Th17 cells. Cold staphylococcal abscesses, retained baby teeth, coarse face, eczema, very high IgE.']]},
{h:'Messengers of the garrison',p:[
'Cytokines are short-range signals. A few broad rules make the table easier: macrophages sound the general alarm (IL-1, IL-6, TNF, IL-8, IL-12); helper T cells give orders (IL-2, IFN-γ, IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, IL-17); and a small set calms the response down (IL-10, TGF-β).'],
table:{cols:['Cytokine','Main source','Main action'],rows:[
['IL-1','macrophages','fever, acute-phase response, endothelial adhesion molecules'],
['IL-2','T cells','growth of T cells, NK cells and Tregs'],
['IL-3','T cells','growth of marrow stem cells'],
['IL-4','Th2 cells','Th2 differentiation, switch to IgE (and IgG4)'],
['IL-5','Th2 cells','eosinophil growth and activation, IgA switch'],
['IL-6','macrophages, T cells','fever, acute-phase proteins (CRP, hepcidin), Th17 differentiation'],
['IL-8 (CXCL8)','macrophages, endothelium','neutrophil chemotaxis'],
['IL-10','Tregs, macrophages','damps down MHC II and Th1 responses'],
['IL-12','macrophages, dendritic cells','Th1 differentiation, NK activation'],
['IL-13','Th2 cells','IgE switch, mucus, airway hyperreactivity'],
['IL-17','Th17 cells','neutrophil recruitment, mucosal defence'],
['TNF-α','macrophages','fever, cachexia, vascular leak, keeps granulomas intact'],
['IFN-α and IFN-β','virus-infected cells, plasmacytoid dendritic cells','antiviral state, more MHC I'],
['IFN-γ','Th1 cells, NK cells','activates macrophages, more MHC I and II, Th1 bias'],
['TGF-β','Tregs and many cells','suppression, fibrosis, IgA switch']]},
key:'Macrophage alarm: IL-1, IL-6 and TNF bring fever; IL-8 calls neutrophils; IL-12 calls Th1. The Th2 trio IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13 serves allergy and worms.',
s:[['pharm','Cytokines given as drugs: aldesleukin (IL-2) for metastatic renal cell carcinoma and melanoma; IFN-β for relapsing multiple sclerosis; IFN-γ to cut infections in chronic granulomatous disease; IFN-α was used for chronic hepatitis B and C. Blocking TNF unseals granulomas, so latent TB must be excluded first.']]},
{h:'Where the garrison lives',p:[
'A **lymph node** is built in zones. The outer cortex holds **follicles** of B cells: primary follicles are dense and quiet, secondary follicles have pale **germinal centres** where B cells proliferate and mature. The deeper **paracortex** holds T cells and the high endothelial venules through which lymphocytes enter from the blood; it is thin in DiGeorge syndrome and swells in viral infections. The **medulla** has medullary cords packed with plasma cells and sinuses lined with macrophages.',
'The **spleen** filters blood rather than lymph. Its **white pulp** has a sheath of T cells around each central arteriole and B-cell follicles beside it. The **marginal zone** between white and red pulp holds macrophages and B cells that respond to bacterial capsules without T-cell help. The **red pulp** removes old or damaged red cells. After splenectomy, Howell-Jolly bodies (nuclear remnants) and target cells appear, and the risk of overwhelming infection from encapsulated organisms (pneumococcus, meningococcus, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Salmonella) stays for life.'],
table:{cols:['Cell','Markers'],rows:[
['All T cells','CD3 (with the T-cell receptor), CD2, CD5, CD7'],
['Helper and cytotoxic T cells','CD4 (binds MHC II); CD8 (binds MHC I)'],
['Regulatory T cells','CD4, CD25 (IL-2 receptor α), FOXP3'],
['B cells','CD19, CD20, CD21 (complement receptor and EBV receptor), CD40, MHC II, B7'],
['NK cells','CD16 (binds IgG Fc), CD56'],
['Macrophages and monocytes','CD14 (LPS receptor), CD40, MHC II, B7, Fc and C3b receptors'],
['Haematopoietic stem cells','CD34']]},
s:[['path','Rituximab targets CD20, which mature B cells carry but plasma cells do not, so antibody levels fall slowly while B cells vanish from the blood within days.']]},
{h:'Antibodies',p:[
'B cells activated with T cell help (CD40 on the B cell binding **CD40L** on the T cell, plus cytokines) undergo **class switching** and **affinity maturation** in germinal centres and become plasma cells and memory cells. Antibody structure: two heavy and two light chains; the **Fab** binds antigen (variable region, from V(D)J recombination by RAG1/2), the **Fc** binds receptors and complement.',
'Protein antigens are **T-dependent**: they give class switching, high-affinity antibody and memory. Polysaccharides such as bacterial capsules are **T-independent**: they cross-link B-cell receptors directly, give mainly IgM, little memory, and work poorly before about age two. The first (primary) response peaks after one to two weeks and is mostly IgM; a second exposure brings a faster, larger, IgG-dominated response from memory cells.'],
table:{cols:['Class','Features'],rows:[
['IgM','first made, pentamer (secreted), best at fixing complement, does not cross the placenta'],
['IgG','most abundant, main secondary response, crosses the placenta, opsonises, fixes complement'],
['IgA','dimer joined by a J chain in secretions; picks up its secretory component crossing the epithelium; breast milk; protects mucosa'],
['IgE','binds mast cells and basophils; allergy and helminths'],
['IgD','on naive B cells; role unclear']]},
s:[['phys','Passive immunity (maternal IgG, immunoglobulin injections, antitoxins) works at once but lasts weeks. Active immunity (infection, vaccines) takes time but leaves memory.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Which cells read peptides on MHC class I?',o:['CD4 T cells','B cells','Neutrophils','CD8 T cells'],a:3,x:'CD8 binds the class I molecule, so cytotoxic T cells survey peptides from inside every nucleated cell. CD4 T cells read class II on antigen-presenting cells.',d:'phys',c:'mhc-tcells'},
{q:'A patient on pembrolizumab develops diarrhoea, and biopsy shows colitis. Mechanism?',o:['Opportunistic infection of the colon','Direct drug toxicity to colonocytes','IgE-mediated drug allergy','Autoimmunity after loss of the PD-1 brake'],a:3,x:'Blocking PD-1 releases T cells from a peripheral tolerance brake, and self-reactive cells attack the gut, liver, thyroid, pituitary or lung. This is an immune-related adverse event treated with steroids, not an infection, although infection must be excluded first.',d:'pharm',c:'mhc-tcells'},
{q:'Which cytokine drives naive CD4 cells to become Th1 cells?',o:['IL-4','IL-10','IL-12','IL-5'],a:2,x:'Macrophages and dendritic cells that meet intracellular microbes release IL-12, which pushes CD4 cells to Th1 and IFN-γ. IL-4 is the tempting choice, but it drives the opposite subset, Th2.',d:'phys',c:'mhc-tcells'},
{q:'Which antibody crosses the placenta?',o:['IgG','IgA','IgE','IgM'],a:0,x:'IgG is actively carried across the placenta by the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), protecting the baby for the first months. IgM is the largest antibody (a pentamer) and cannot cross, so fetal IgM against an infection means the fetus made it.',d:'phys',c:'antibodies'},
{q:'Before abacavir is started, which test is needed?',o:['HLA-B27','HLA-DR4','HLA-B*57:01','HLA-DQ2'],a:2,x:'HLA-B*57:01 carriers can have a dangerous hypersensitivity reaction (fever, rash, gut and breathing symptoms), so abacavir is avoided if the test is positive. HLA-B*15:02 is the test before carbamazepine, not abacavir.',d:'pharm',c:'hiv'},
{q:'A lymph node biopsy from an infant with hypocalcaemia and truncus arteriosus shows normal follicles but a nearly empty zone between them and the medulla. Which zone is empty?',o:['Follicles and germinal centres','Paracortex','Medullary cords','Subcapsular sinus'],a:1,x:'The paracortex is the T-cell zone, and in DiGeorge syndrome the absent thymus means few T cells reach it. Follicles hold B cells, which are present, although antibody quality suffers without T-cell help.',d:'path',c:'mhc-tcells'},
{q:'A patient with severe eosinophilic asthma starts mepolizumab. Which cytokine does it block, and which cell makes it?',o:['IL-4 from macrophages','IL-5 from Th2 cells','IL-17 from Th17 cells','IFN-γ from Th1 cells'],a:1,x:'Mepolizumab binds IL-5, the Th2 cytokine that grows and activates eosinophils, so attacks and steroid needs fall. IL-4 is also a Th2 cytokine (made by T cells, not macrophages) and is blocked through its receptor by dupilumab.',d:'pharm',c:'asthma'}
]};

BOOK['c4-hyper']={depth:4,title:'The Overreaction',
epi:'These guards were trained to strike and no longer know when to stop. They strike at dust, at pollen, at the walls they were raised to defend.',
c:['hypersensitivity','autoimmunity','nephritic-nephrotic','thyroid','nmj'],
S:[
{h:'Four types',p:[
'Hypersensitivity reactions are immune responses that damage the host. The classic scheme sorts them by the arm of the immune system that does the harm. A simple mnemonic for the types is **ACID**: Anaphylactic (I), Cytotoxic (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed (IV). Types I to III use antibodies; type IV uses T cells. Timing is a strong clue: type I acts in minutes, type II over hours to days, type III a week or more after a large antigen load (or hours for a local Arthus reaction), and type IV over two to three days.',
'Many real diseases mix types. Asthma has an early type I phase and a late, T-cell-driven inflammation; lupus is mainly type III but also has type II cytopenias. The scheme still helps, because each type predicts the tests that will be abnormal and the drugs that will help.'],
table:{cols:['Type','Mechanism','Examples'],rows:[
['I, immediate','IgE on mast cells cross-linked by allergen → histamine, tryptase (immediate), leukotrienes and prostaglandins (late phase)','anaphylaxis, allergic asthma, hay fever, urticaria'],
['II, cytotoxic','IgG or IgM against antigens on cells or tissue → complement, phagocytosis, ADCC; or receptor blocking/stimulating','autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, transfusion reactions, Goodpasture (anti-collagen IV), rheumatic fever, pemphigus, bullous pemphigoid, myasthenia gravis, Graves'],
['III, immune complex','antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessels, joints, glomeruli → complement, neutrophils','SLE, post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, serum sickness, polyarteritis nodosa, Arthus reaction'],
['IV, delayed','sensitised T cells: CD4 Th1 releasing cytokines, or CD8 killing','contact dermatitis (nickel, poison ivy), tuberculin test, type 1 diabetes, multiple sclerosis, transplant rejection']]},
key:'Coombs test positive → type II. Low complement with vasculitis or nephritis → often type III. No antibody at all, and a 48-hour delay → type IV.'},
{h:'Type I: the second meeting',p:[
'Type I needs two meetings with the allergen. At the first, dendritic cells present it to naive CD4 cells, which become **Th2** cells in people prone to allergy (**atopy**, a strongly inherited trait). Th2 cells release **IL-4 and IL-13**, which switch B cells to make **IgE**. The IgE binds high-affinity receptors (FcεRI) on **mast cells** in the skin, airways and gut, and on basophils in the blood. Nothing is felt: the person is now sensitised.',
'At the second meeting the allergen cross-links IgE on the mast cell surface, and the cell empties its granules within minutes. **Preformed** contents act first: histamine (vasodilation, leaky venules, itch, bronchospasm), tryptase and heparin. Over the next hours the cell makes **new lipid mediators**, leukotrienes C₄, D₄ and E₄ (strong, lasting bronchoconstriction and mucus), prostaglandin D₂ and platelet-activating factor, and releases cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, TNF). These draw in **eosinophils** and Th2 cells for a **late phase** 2 to 24 hours later, which is what keeps asthma and allergic rhinitis going after the trigger has gone.',
'**Anaphylaxis** is this reaction spread through the body: urticaria and angioedema, wheeze and laryngeal swelling, hypotension from vasodilation and leak, vomiting and cramps. Some reactions look identical but need no IgE, so they can happen on the first dose: opioids, vancomycin (the infusion flushing reaction) and radiocontrast trigger mast cells directly, and NSAIDs can provoke wheeze and swelling by shifting arachidonic acid towards leukotrienes. Serum **tryptase** peaks about 1 to 2 hours after the reaction and confirms mast cell activation. Skin-prick testing and specific IgE identify the allergen later.'],
key:'Minutes: preformed granules (histamine, tryptase). Hours: freshly made lipids and eosinophils (the late phase). Tryptase is the mast cell\'s fingerprint.',
s:[['pharm','Anaphylaxis: intramuscular adrenaline 0.5 mg (1 mg/mL) into the anterolateral thigh in adults, repeated after 5 minutes if needed. Antihistamines and steroids are secondary. If a patient on a β-blocker does not respond to adrenaline, glucagon raises heart rate and contractility through its own receptor, bypassing the blocked β receptors.'],['phys','Why adrenaline works: α₁ effects constrict vessels and reduce swelling, β₁ effects raise heart rate and contractility, and β₂ effects open the airways and stabilise mast cells so they release less.'],['bio','Drugs aimed at each step of type I: H₁ antihistamines block histamine; cromolyn stabilises mast cells; montelukast blocks the leukotriene receptor and zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase; omalizumab binds free IgE; mepolizumab and benralizumab target IL-5 and its receptor; dupilumab blocks the IL-4 receptor α chain, shutting off both IL-4 and IL-13. Allergen immunotherapy shifts the response towards blocking IgG4 and regulatory T cells.']]},
{h:'Type II: antibodies against fixed targets',p:[
'In type II the antigen is fixed to a cell or to tissue: a red cell antigen, a basement membrane protein, a receptor. IgG or IgM binds it and harms it in one of four ways. **Opsonisation and phagocytosis**: coated cells are eaten by splenic macrophages (warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, immune thrombocytopenia, haemolytic disease of the newborn). **Complement and inflammation**: complement lyses the cell or draws in neutrophils that injure the tissue (ABO transfusion reactions, Goodpasture syndrome, pemphigus vulgaris, bullous pemphigoid). **Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity**: NK cells bind the Fc of IgG on the target and kill it. **Receptor dysfunction without cell death**: the antibody switches a receptor on (Graves disease: TSH receptor) or blocks it and has it removed (myasthenia gravis: acetylcholine receptor; pernicious anaemia: intrinsic factor).',
'How tolerance is lost can often be named. In **rheumatic fever**, antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin and valve tissue (molecular mimicry). In drug-induced haemolysis, a drug such as penicillin binds the red cell membrane and becomes the target (a hapten), or a drug such as methyldopa induces true autoantibodies.'],
key:'Direct Coombs: antibody or complement is already on the patient\'s red cells. Indirect Coombs: free antibody waits in the serum (crossmatching, antenatal screening).',
s:[['path','Immunofluorescence tells type II from type III in the kidney. Linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane means antibody bound to a fixed antigen (type II, Goodpasture: collagen IV α3). Lumpy, granular deposits mean immune complexes trapped from the blood (type III: post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, lupus).'],['micro','In the blistering diseases the depth of the split names the target. Pemphigus vulgaris: antibodies against desmoglein 3 and 1 separate keratinocytes inside the epidermis (fragile blisters, mouth ulcers, Nikolsky sign positive). Bullous pemphigoid: antibodies against hemidesmosome proteins (BP180, BP230) lift the whole epidermis (tense blisters, mouth spared, Nikolsky negative).']]},
{h:'Type III: complexes that lodge',p:[
'Antigen and antibody always form complexes, and most are cleared harmlessly: large ones are taken up by macrophages, and red cells carry small ones (bound by complement receptor 1) to the liver and spleen. Disease follows when complexes of middle size form in slight antigen excess, escape clearance and lodge where blood is filtered under pressure: **glomeruli, synovium, small vessels of the skin, and serosa**. There they bind complement. C3 and C4 are used up, so their blood levels **fall**, and C5a draws in neutrophils, whose enzymes cause **fibrinoid necrosis** of vessel walls and a leukocytoclastic vasculitis. Because the complexes travel, type III disease is usually **systemic**, unlike type II, which strikes where its antigen is.',
'**Serum sickness** shows the timing. About 5 to 10 days after a large dose of foreign protein (antivenom, antithymocyte globulin, some monoclonal antibodies) the body makes antibody while antigen is still plentiful. Fever, an urticarial or vasculitic rash, joint pain, lymph node swelling and proteinuria follow, with low complement; the illness settles as the antigen is cleared. Some drugs, such as cefaclor and penicillins, cause a similar serum sickness-like reaction. The **Arthus reaction** is the local version: in a person who already has high antibody levels, an injected antigen (for example a tetanus booster given too soon) forms complexes in the skin wall, giving painful swelling and sometimes necrosis within 4 to 12 hours.',
'Infections can supply the antigen: hepatitis B in polyarteritis nodosa, hepatitis C in mixed cryoglobulinaemia, streptococcal antigens in post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, bacteria in endocarditis-related glomerulonephritis.'],
s:[['phys','Complement in the glomerular diseases: C3 is low in post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, lupus nephritis, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, cryoglobulinaemia (C4 especially) and endocarditis; it is normal in IgA nephropathy, IgA vasculitis, Goodpasture syndrome and ANCA vasculitis.']]},
{h:'Type IV: the slow hand of T cells',p:[
'Type IV involves no antibody, so it cannot be passed on with serum, only with T cells. It takes **48 to 72 hours**, because memory T cells must be recruited to the site, activated and allowed to call in macrophages. There are two arms. **CD4 Th1 cells** release IFN-γ, which activates macrophages: the tuberculin skin test, and at its most persistent, the granuloma. **CD8 cells** kill target cells directly: contact dermatitis, virus-infected liver cells in hepatitis, islet cells in type 1 diabetes, and graft cells in acute cellular rejection.',
'In **contact dermatitis** a small molecule (nickel, the urushiol of poison ivy, neomycin, fragrances) binds skin proteins and becomes antigenic (a hapten). Langerhans cells carry it to the lymph node. At the next contact the T cells return and an itchy, vesicular rash appears in the exact shape of the contact two days later. **Patch testing** reproduces this. The most dangerous drug rashes are also T-cell reactions: Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (CD8 cells killing keratinocytes) and DRESS, with fever, eosinophilia and organ injury weeks after the drug starts.',
'A delayed skin test can be falsely negative (**anergy**) when T cells are suppressed: HIV, sarcoidosis, high-dose steroids, overwhelming TB, malnutrition and recent measles infection. Interferon-γ release assays measure the same Th1 memory in a blood sample and are not affected by earlier BCG vaccination.'],
s:[['pharm','Common causes of Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis: allopurinol (HLA-B*58:01), carbamazepine and phenytoin (HLA-B*15:02), lamotrigine, sulfonamides, nevirapine and NSAIDs of the oxicam group. Stop the drug at once; the earlier it stops, the better the outcome.'],['micro','Measles and live measles vaccine suppress T-cell responses for weeks, so a tuberculin test should be placed on the same day as MMR or at least 4 weeks after it.']]},
{h:'How tolerance breaks',p:[
'Self-reactive lymphocytes are made every day. **Central tolerance** deletes most of them in the thymus (helped by AIRE) and bone marrow. **Peripheral tolerance** controls the escapees: T cells that meet antigen without co-stimulation become anergic, regulatory T cells (FOXP3) suppress neighbours with IL-10 and TGF-β, and CTLA-4 and PD-1 apply brakes. Autoimmunity follows when these controls fail in a person with the wrong genes who meets the wrong trigger.',
'**Genes**: HLA alleles (HLA-B27, DR3, DR4), and immune genes such as PTPN22, CTLA-4 and complement genes. **Sex**: most autoimmune diseases are far commoner in women, through oestrogen effects and immune genes on the X chromosome. **Triggers**: **molecular mimicry** (rheumatic fever after streptococci; Guillain-Barré syndrome after Campylobacter), release of antigens normally hidden from the immune system (sympathetic ophthalmia after injury to one eye; anti-sperm antibodies after vasectomy), and tissue damage that exposes new self-antigens. Once started, the response often broadens to new parts of the same tissue (**epitope spreading**). Many autoimmune diseases have a characteristic antibody.'],
table:{cols:['Antibody','Disease'],rows:[
['ANA','SLE (sensitive in about 95%, not specific)'],['anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith','SLE (specific); dsDNA tracks nephritis'],['anti-histone','drug-induced lupus (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid)'],
['anti-CCP, rheumatoid factor','rheumatoid arthritis (anti-CCP more specific)'],['anti-centromere','limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)'],['anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I)','diffuse systemic sclerosis'],
['anti-Ro (SSA), anti-La (SSB)','Sjögren; anti-Ro: neonatal lupus, congenital heart block'],['anti-Jo-1, anti-Mi-2','polymyositis, dermatomyositis'],['anti-U1 RNP','mixed connective tissue disease'],
['c-ANCA (PR3)','granulomatosis with polyangiitis'],['p-ANCA (MPO)','microscopic polyangiitis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis'],
['anti-glomerular basement membrane','Goodpasture syndrome'],['anti-tissue transglutaminase, anti-endomysial','coeliac disease'],
['anti-TSH receptor','Graves disease'],['anti-TPO','Hashimoto thyroiditis'],['anti-acetylcholine receptor','myasthenia gravis'],['anti-voltage-gated calcium channel','Lambert-Eaton syndrome'],
['anti-desmoglein 3 and 1','pemphigus vulgaris'],['anti-hemidesmosome (BP180, BP230)','bullous pemphigoid'],['anti-intrinsic factor, anti-parietal cell','pernicious anaemia'],
['anti-aquaporin-4','neuromyelitis optica'],['anti-NMDA receptor','autoimmune encephalitis (sometimes with an ovarian teratoma)'],
['anti-cardiolipin, lupus anticoagulant, anti-β₂-glycoprotein I','antiphospholipid syndrome'],
['anti-mitochondrial','primary biliary cholangitis'],['anti-smooth muscle','autoimmune hepatitis'],['anti-GAD, anti-insulin','type 1 diabetes'],['anti-phospholipase A2 receptor','primary membranous nephropathy']]}},
{h:'Lupus',p:[
'Systemic lupus erythematosus is a type III disease of immune complexes against nuclear antigens, mostly in women of childbearing age, and commoner and more severe in people of African, Asian and Hispanic ancestry. It begins with poor disposal of dying cells: ultraviolet light and other injuries release nuclear material, which is not cleared fast enough (early complement deficiency is one cause). Plasmacytoid dendritic cells respond with large amounts of type I interferon, B cells make antibodies against DNA and nuclear proteins, and the resulting complexes deposit in skin, joints, kidneys and serosa.',
'Features: malar rash sparing the nasolabial folds, photosensitivity, discoid lesions, painless mouth ulcers, non-erosive arthritis, serositis, nephritis, cytopenias (type II antibodies against blood cells), neuropsychiatric disease. Lupus nephritis is graded I to VI on biopsy: diffuse proliferative nephritis (class IV, with wire-loop lesions and a nephritic picture) is the most common serious form; membranous nephritis (class V) gives nephrotic syndrome. Labs: **ANA**, **anti-dsDNA**, **low C3 and C4** in flares. Common causes of death are kidney disease, infection and cardiovascular disease, which lupus accelerates.',
'**Drug-induced lupus** (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid, minocycline, TNF inhibitors) gives arthritis, serositis and anti-histone antibodies, rarely affects the kidney or brain, and fades after the drug stops. **Libman-Sacks endocarditis** (sterile vegetations on both sides of the mitral valve). **Antiphospholipid syndrome** (anticardiolipin, lupus anticoagulant, anti-β₂-glycoprotein I): venous and arterial thrombosis, recurrent miscarriage, a falsely prolonged PTT and false-positive syphilis test. Anti-Ro antibodies cross the placenta and can damage the fetal conduction system: congenital heart block.'],
s:[['pharm','Hydroxychloroquine for nearly all patients (check eyes for retinopathy). Steroids, mycophenolate or cyclophosphamide for nephritis; belimumab (anti-BAFF), anifrolumab (blocks the type I interferon receptor) and voclosporin (a calcineurin inhibitor, for nephritis) are newer options. Antiphospholipid syndrome: warfarin long term; direct oral anticoagulants are avoided in high-risk (triple-positive) disease; low-molecular-weight heparin with low-dose aspirin in pregnancy.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A positive direct Coombs test and spherocytes after starting a new drug. Which hypersensitivity type?',o:['I','II','III','IV'],a:1,x:'Antibody bound to red cells, detected by the direct Coombs test, marks the cells for splenic macrophages, which nibble them into spherocytes: type II. Type III also involves antibody, but as circulating complexes that lodge in vessels, not antibody fixed to a cell.',d:'path',c:'hypersensitivity'},
{q:'Fever, rash, joint pain and proteinuria 10 days after antivenom. Type and mechanism?',o:['Type I, IgE','Type II, anti-cell antibodies','Type III, immune complexes','Type IV, T cells'],a:2,x:'Serum sickness: about a week after a large load of foreign protein, new antibody forms complexes with the remaining antigen, which deposit in skin, joints and glomeruli and consume complement. Type I would appear within minutes of the infusion, not 10 days later.',d:'path',c:'hypersensitivity'},
{q:'Which antibody best tracks lupus nephritis activity?',o:['ANA','anti-Smith','anti-histone','anti-dsDNA'],a:3,x:'Anti-dsDNA levels rise, and C3 and C4 fall, as nephritis flares, so they are followed over time. Anti-Smith is just as specific for lupus but does not change with disease activity.',d:'path',c:'autoimmunity'},
{q:'A woman with recurrent miscarriages, a DVT and a prolonged PTT that does not correct with mixing. Diagnosis?',o:['Antiphospholipid syndrome','Factor VIII deficiency','von Willebrand disease','Vitamin K deficiency'],a:0,x:'Lupus anticoagulant blocks the phospholipid the test needs, so the PTT is prolonged in the tube and does not correct with normal plasma, yet in the body it causes clots. Factor VIII deficiency also prolongs the PTT, but it corrects with mixing and causes bleeding, not thrombosis.',d:'path',c:'autoimmunity'},
{q:'Nickel earrings cause an itchy rash after 48 hours. Mechanism?',o:['IgE on mast cells','IgG against keratinocytes','Immune complexes','Sensitised T cells'],a:3,x:'Nickel acts as a hapten, and memory T cells return to the skin over two days: type IV contact dermatitis. An IgE-mediated rash would be urticarial and appear within minutes.',d:'path',c:'hypersensitivity'},
{q:'A man taking propranolol has anaphylaxis after a wasp sting. Two doses of intramuscular adrenaline fail to lift his blood pressure. Which drug should be added?',o:['Atropine','Glucagon','Hydrocortisone','Chlorphenamine'],a:1,x:'Glucagon raises cAMP in the heart through its own receptor, bypassing the β-blockade that is blunting adrenaline. Hydrocortisone and antihistamines are adjuncts that act too slowly, or too weakly, to rescue the blood pressure.',d:'pharm',c:'hypersensitivity'},
{q:'A man has haemoptysis and haematuria. Kidney biopsy shows linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane. Mechanism?',o:['IgE-mediated mast cell release','Immune complexes trapped from the blood','Antibody bound to a fixed antigen in the membrane','T cells activating macrophages'],a:2,x:'Goodpasture syndrome: antibody against collagen IV α3 binds evenly along the basement membrane of kidney and lung, giving a smooth linear pattern (type II). Trapped immune complexes give a granular, lumpy pattern instead.',d:'path',c:'nephritic-nephrotic'}
]};

BOOK['c4-deficient']={depth:4,title:'The Empty Barracks',
epi:'A keep can stand for years with whole barracks unmanned. The enemies that walk in tell you which rooms are empty.',
c:['hiv','immunodeficiency'],
S:[
{h:'Reading the infection pattern',p:[
'The organisms a patient catches point to the missing part, because each arm of the immune system has its own work. **Antibodies** neutralise viruses and toxins and opsonise bacteria that hide behind capsules; when they fail, the result is recurrent sinusitis, otitis and pneumonia with encapsulated bacteria (pneumococcus, Haemophilus influenzae), enteroviral infection, and Giardia in the gut. These start after about **6 months**, when the maternal IgG that crossed the placenta has faded.',
'**T cells** control anything that lives inside cells. Their loss lets viruses (CMV, varicella, adenovirus), fungi (Candida, Pneumocystis), intracellular bacteria and even live vaccines run unchecked, and the baby fails to thrive from the first weeks. Because B cells need T-cell help to make good antibody, a severe T-cell defect is usually a **combined** defect. **Phagocyte** defects give deep abscesses with Staphylococcus aureus, Gram-negative rods and moulds, plus poor wound healing and gum disease. **Complement** defects give Neisseria (terminal components), encapsulated bacteria (C3) or immune complex disease (early components).',
'Testing follows the same logic: a full blood count with differential (lymphopenia in combined defects, giant granules or neutrophilia in phagocyte defects), immunoglobulin levels and vaccine antibody responses, lymphocyte subsets by flow cytometry, total complement activity (CH50 and AH50), and the dihydrorhodamine test of the respiratory burst. Newborn screening for severe combined immunodeficiency counts **TRECs**, circles of DNA cut out when T-cell receptors are made in the thymus; few TRECs mean few new T cells.'],
table:{cols:['Missing arm','Typical infections','Onset','First tests'],rows:[
['B cells and antibody','encapsulated bacteria, enteroviruses, Giardia','after 6 months','IgG, IgA, IgM; vaccine responses; B-cell count'],
['T cells (or combined)','viruses, Candida, Pneumocystis, intracellular bacteria, live vaccines','first weeks; failure to thrive','lymphocyte count, CD3/CD4/CD8 subsets, TRECs'],
['Phagocytes','Staphylococcus, Gram-negatives, Aspergillus; abscesses','any age','neutrophil count and look; dihydrorhodamine test; CD18 by flow'],
['Complement','Neisseria; encapsulated bacteria; lupus-like disease','any age','CH50, AH50, single components']]},
key:'Antibody gaps open after 6 months, when the mother\'s IgG runs out. T-cell gaps open in the first weeks and bring fungi and viruses.'},
{h:'When the antibody factories fail',p:[
'In **X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinaemia** a faulty Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) stops B-cell precursors from maturing past the pre-B stage in the marrow. There are no mature B cells, so there are no follicles: tonsils and lymph nodes are tiny or absent, and every immunoglobulin class is very low. Boys present after 6 months with pneumonia, otitis and sinusitis, and are at risk of chronic enteroviral meningoencephalitis and of paralysis from live oral polio vaccine.',
'**Selective IgA deficiency** is the commonest primary immunodeficiency (IgA below 7 mg/dL with normal IgG and IgM). Most people are well. Some have sinopulmonary or gut infections (Giardia), autoimmune disease or coeliac disease, and a few form anti-IgA antibodies that cause anaphylaxis when blood products containing IgA are given. **Common variable immunodeficiency** is a failure of B cells to become plasma cells; B cells are usually present but IgG and IgA (and often IgM) are low and vaccine responses are poor. It presents in the teens to the 40s with recurrent infections and bronchiectasis, and with autoimmune cytopenias, granulomas and a raised risk of lymphoma and gastric cancer.',
'In **hyper-IgM syndrome** the usual fault is CD40 ligand on T cells (X-linked). Without CD40-CD40L contact, B cells cannot class switch: IgM is normal or high while IgG, IgA and IgE are low. CD40L also lets T cells activate macrophages, so these children catch Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium (which can scar the bile ducts) and CMV, as in a T-cell defect.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Defect','Clues'],rows:[
['X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinaemia','BTK: B cells cannot mature','boys after 6 months, no tonsils or lymph nodes, all immunoglobulins low, no B cells; no live polio vaccine'],
['Selective IgA deficiency','IgA not made','commonest; often silent; mucosal infections, coeliac and autoimmune disease; anaphylaxis to transfusions containing IgA; check total IgA with coeliac serology, because IgA anti-tTG is falsely negative'],
['Common variable immunodeficiency','B cells fail to become plasma cells','teens to adults; low IgG and IgA, often IgM; poor vaccine responses; bronchiectasis, autoimmunity, granulomas, lymphoma'],
['Hyper-IgM syndrome','CD40L (usually X-linked)','high or normal IgM, low IgG, IgA, IgE; Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium, CMV'],
['Transient hypogammaglobulinaemia of infancy','slow start of IgG production','low IgG at 6–12 months with normal vaccine responses; recovers by 2–4 years']]},
s:[['pharm','Antibody deficiencies (Bruton, CVID, hyper-IgM) are treated with lifelong immunoglobulin, intravenous or subcutaneous, plus prompt antibiotics. Selective IgA deficiency needs no replacement, and immunoglobulin products, which contain traces of IgA, can provoke anaphylaxis in those with anti-IgA antibodies; IgA-deficient or washed blood products are used.']]},
{h:'When the T cells never arrive',p:[
'**DiGeorge syndrome** (22q11.2 deletion) leaves the thymus small or absent, so T cells are few: no thymic shadow on the chest film, an empty paracortex in lymph nodes, hypocalcaemic tetany from absent parathyroids, and conotruncal heart defects. Most children have a partial form and slowly improve; the rare complete form needs thymus tissue transplant.',
'**Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)** is a group of defects that leave no working T cells. The commonest form is X-linked: the **common γ chain** (IL2RG) is shared by the receptors for IL-2, 4, 7, 9, 15 and 21, and without IL-7 and IL-15 signals there are no T cells and no NK cells, while B cells are present but useless without T-cell help. **Adenosine deaminase deficiency** (autosomal recessive) lets deoxyadenosine and dATP build up and poison dividing lymphocytes, so T, B and NK cells are all low. **RAG1 or RAG2** defects stop V(D)J recombination, so neither T nor B cells form but NK cells remain. Babies have thrush, chronic diarrhoea, failure to thrive and severe infections, often including disseminated BCG. Stem cell transplant is curative; enzyme replacement (PEG-ADA) and gene therapy exist for ADA deficiency. Until then, no live vaccines, and blood products must be irradiated and CMV-negative.',
'Two inherited syndromes (one X-linked, one autosomal recessive) combine immune failure with other signs. **Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome** (WAS protein, which organises the actin skeleton of blood cells): eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, recurrent infections with encapsulated bacteria from poor polysaccharide responses, autoimmunity and lymphoma; IgM is low, IgA and IgE high. **Ataxia telangiectasia** (ATM, which senses double-strand DNA breaks): cerebellar ataxia from the toddler years, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, IgA and IgG2 deficiency, sinopulmonary infections, leukaemia and lymphoma, raised AFP, and sensitivity to radiation.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Defect','Clues'],rows:[
['DiGeorge syndrome','no thymus (22q11.2)','T-cell deficiency, hypocalcaemia, conotruncal heart defects, no thymic shadow'],
['SCID, X-linked','IL-2R common γ chain','T and NK cells absent, B cells present; thrush, diarrhoea, failure to thrive; low TRECs'],
['SCID, ADA deficiency','adenosine deaminase (autosomal recessive)','T, B and NK cells all low; toxic deoxyadenosine; cupped rib ends'],
['SCID, RAG1/RAG2','no V(D)J recombination','T and B absent, NK present; hypomorphic form (Omenn syndrome) gives red scaly skin and eosinophilia'],
['Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome','WAS (X-linked)','eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, infections; low IgM, high IgA and IgE'],
['Ataxia telangiectasia','ATM (DNA repair)','ataxia, oculocutaneous telangiectasias (conjunctivae first), IgA deficiency, sinopulmonary infections, lymphoma and leukaemia, raised AFP, radiation sensitivity'],
['Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis','T-cell response to Candida (AIRE, STAT1)','persistent skin, nail and mouth Candida'],
['IL-12 or IFN-γ receptor defects','Th1 and macrophage loop broken','disseminated mycobacterial (including BCG) and Salmonella infections']]},
s:[['pharm','Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, oral polio, yellow fever, BCG, rotavirus, live typhoid) are contraindicated in severe T-cell and combined deficiencies. Antibody deficiencies need IV or subcutaneous immunoglobulin.'],['bio','ADA and purine nucleoside phosphorylase both work in purine salvage. Without ADA, deoxyadenosine is phosphorylated to dATP, which inhibits ribonucleotide reductase; lymphocytes, which divide fast and lean on this pathway, cannot make DNA.']]},
{h:'When the phagocytes cannot finish',p:[
'A neutrophil must roll on selectins, stick with integrins, squeeze through the vessel wall, engulf, and then kill inside the phagolysosome. A break at any step leaves bacteria alive. In **leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1** (CD18, the β₂ integrin chain), neutrophils cannot stick and leave the blood: the white count is very high, the umbilical cord separates late (after about 30 days), infections form no pus, gums and wounds heal badly.',
'In **chronic granulomatous disease** the NADPH oxidase cannot make superoxide (the X-linked gp91phox form is commonest). Phagocytes can still borrow hydrogen peroxide made by the microbe itself, unless the microbe destroys it with **catalase**. So the infections are with catalase-positive organisms: Staphylococcus aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia cepacia, Nocardia and Aspergillus, forming abscesses and granulomas. The **dihydrorhodamine** flow test shows no fluorescence after stimulation (the older nitroblue tetrazolium test stays colourless).',
'**Chédiak-Higashi syndrome** (LYST, the lysosomal trafficking regulator; autosomal recessive) disrupts how granules and lysosomes move and fuse. Neutrophils carry giant granules but cannot kill well; melanosomes fail too (partial albinism, silvery hair); platelets bleed; peripheral neuropathy develops; and many children enter a fatal haemophagocytic phase without a stem cell transplant. **Myeloperoxidase deficiency** is common and usually silent: the respiratory burst is normal, but the final step to hypochlorite fails, and Candida infections can occur, mainly in people with diabetes.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Defect','Clues'],rows:[
['Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1','CD18 (β₂ integrin)','late cord separation, omphalitis without pus, very high neutrophil count, periodontitis'],
['Chronic granulomatous disease','NADPH oxidase','catalase-positive infections, abscesses and granulomas; abnormal dihydrorhodamine test'],
['Chédiak-Higashi syndrome','LYST','giant granules, partial albinism, neuropathy, bleeding, pyogenic infections'],
['Myeloperoxidase deficiency','myeloperoxidase','usually silent; Candida in diabetes; respiratory burst test normal']]},
s:[['micro','In chronic granulomatous disease prophylaxis is trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole against bacteria, itraconazole against Aspergillus, and interferon-γ to boost the remaining killing. Aspergillus is the leading cause of death.']]},
{h:'HIV: the acquired deficiency',p:[
'HIV is an enveloped retrovirus with two copies of single-stranded RNA. Its **gag** gene makes the structural core (p24 capsid, p17 matrix), **pol** makes reverse transcriptase, integrase and protease, and **env** makes gp160, which a host protease cuts into **gp120** and **gp41**. It spreads through sex, blood and from mother to child.',
'HIV binds **CD4** with gp120 and uses a co-receptor: **CCR5** early (macrophage-tropic; homozygous CCR5Δ32 confers resistance) and CXCR4 later (T-cell-tropic). gp41 mediates fusion. Reverse transcriptase copies its RNA into DNA, making many errors, which is why resistance arises quickly; integrase inserts the DNA into host DNA, and protease cuts the polyproteins into mature viral parts.',
'After acute infection (a mononucleosis-like illness at 2 to 4 weeks, with fever, sore throat, lymph node swelling, rash and mouth ulcers, and a very high viral load), viral load falls to a set point and CD4 counts decline slowly over years while the virus keeps replicating in lymphoid tissue. Without treatment, AIDS follows after about 10 years on average. **AIDS** is a CD4 count below 200 cells/µL or an AIDS-defining illness. Babies born to mothers with HIV carry maternal antibodies for up to 18 months, so infants are tested by HIV nucleic acid tests, not antibody tests.'],
table:{cols:['CD4 count','Opportunistic infections','Prophylaxis'],rows:[
['< 500','oral thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia (EBV), shingles, TB (at any count), Kaposi sarcoma (HHV-8), recurrent bacterial pneumonia','—'],
['< 200','Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus; non-enhancing white-matter lesions), HIV-associated dementia, Cryptosporidium diarrhoea','trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole'],
['< 100','Toxoplasma brain abscesses (multiple, ring-enhancing), cryptococcal meningitis, Histoplasma, oesophageal Candida, bacillary angiomatosis (Bartonella)','TMP-SMX if Toxoplasma IgG positive; screen serum cryptococcal antigen'],
['< 50','CMV retinitis and colitis, Mycobacterium avium complex, primary CNS lymphoma (EBV; usually a single lesion)','azithromycin no longer routine if ART is started at once']]},
s:[['micro','Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome: worsening of a known or hidden infection weeks after starting ART, as the recovering immune system attacks it.']]},
{h:'Holding the virus back',p:[
'Antiretroviral therapy (ART) is started for everyone with HIV, at any CD4 count, as soon as possible. A usual regimen is two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors plus an integrase inhibitor; resistance testing is done first. Viral load should become undetectable within months, and a person with a durably undetectable viral load does not pass HIV on through sex.',
'Prevention uses the same drugs. **Post-exposure prophylaxis** starts within 72 hours of an exposure and lasts 28 days. **Pre-exposure prophylaxis** is daily tenofovir with emtricitabine or long-acting injections (cabotegravir every two months, lenacapavir every six months). In pregnancy, ART for the mother and a short course for the newborn cut transmission to below 1%.'],
table:{cols:['Class','Examples','Key toxicities'],rows:[
['Entry inhibitors','maraviroc (blocks CCR5), enfuvirtide (binds gp41)','maraviroc only works on CCR5-tropic virus (test first); enfuvirtide injection-site reactions'],
['Nucleoside RT inhibitors','tenofovir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, abacavir, zidovudine','class: lactic acidosis; zidovudine anaemia and marrow suppression; abacavir hypersensitivity (HLA-B*57:01); tenofovir disoproxil kidney tubule damage and bone loss'],
['Non-nucleoside RT inhibitors','efavirenz, rilpivirine, nevirapine','efavirenz vivid dreams and psychiatric effects; nevirapine rash and liver failure'],
['Integrase inhibitors','dolutegravir, bictegravir, raltegravir, cabotegravir','weight gain; dolutegravir raises creatinine by blocking its tubular secretion, without lowering true GFR'],
['Protease inhibitors','darunavir, atazanavir (names end in -navir)','hyperglycaemia, lipodystrophy, many interactions; atazanavir causes harmless unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia'],
['Boosters','ritonavir, cobicistat','inhibit CYP3A4 to raise levels of other drugs'],
['Capsid inhibitor','lenacapavir','injection-site nodules']]},
s:[['pharm','Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors lack a 3\'-OH group, so once built into viral DNA they stop the chain. Non-nucleoside inhibitors bind a separate pocket on reverse transcriptase and need no activation. Rifampicin induces CYP3A4 and lowers the levels of protease inhibitors and some integrase inhibitors, so TB and HIV regimens must be matched.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 9-month-old boy has recurrent otitis and pneumonia. Tonsils are absent and he has no B cells in the blood. Defect?',o:['CD40 ligand','Adenosine deaminase','ATM','Bruton tyrosine kinase'],a:3,x:'BTK is needed for pre-B cells to mature, so there are no B cells, no tonsils and all immunoglobulins are low; infections start at about 6 months as maternal IgG fades. A CD40 ligand defect (hyper-IgM) leaves B cells present with normal or high IgM, and ADA deficiency is SCID, with T cells missing too.',d:'path',c:'immunodeficiency'},
{q:'A man has anaphylaxis during his first blood transfusion. Which deficiency is most likely?',o:['IgA','IgG','C3','IgE'],a:0,x:'In selective IgA deficiency some people form anti-IgA antibodies, which react with the IgA in donor plasma; IgA-deficient or washed products prevent it. IgE deficiency would make allergic reactions less likely, not more.',d:'path',c:'immunodeficiency'},
{q:'A boy with eczema, bloody stools from thrombocytopenia with small platelets and recurrent otitis. Diagnosis?',o:['Chédiak-Higashi syndrome','Hyper-IgE syndrome','Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome','DiGeorge syndrome'],a:2,x:'Eczema, small platelets and infections together are Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, an X-linked defect of the actin skeleton of blood cells. Hyper-IgE syndrome also has eczema, but with cold staphylococcal abscesses and normal platelets.',d:'path',c:'immunodeficiency'},
{q:'A man with HIV and a CD4 count of 70 cells/µL has headache and several ring-enhancing brain lesions. Most likely?',o:['Cryptococcus neoformans','Toxoplasma gondii','CMV','JC virus (progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy)'],a:1,x:'Multiple ring-enhancing lesions below 100 CD4 cells suggest Toxoplasma, which usually responds to treatment within two weeks; primary CNS lymphoma (often single and EBV positive) is the main alternative. JC virus causes non-enhancing white-matter lesions without mass effect.',d:'micro',c:'hiv'},
{q:'At what CD4 count is Pneumocystis prophylaxis started?',o:['Below 500','Below 200','Below 100','Below 50'],a:1,x:'Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is started below 200 cells/µL (or with oral thrush), and also covers Toxoplasma when the count falls below 100. Below 50 is the threshold that once triggered MAC prophylaxis.',d:'pharm',c:'hiv'},
{q:'A 4-year-old with silvery hair and pale skin has recurrent skin abscesses, easy bruising and a new peripheral neuropathy. The blood film shows giant granules in neutrophils. Which gene is defective?',o:['NADPH oxidase','CD18 integrin','LYST','Myeloperoxidase'],a:2,x:'Chédiak-Higashi syndrome: the LYST defect disrupts granule and lysosome trafficking in neutrophils, melanocytes, platelets and nerves. NADPH oxidase deficiency (chronic granulomatous disease) also causes abscesses, but with normal granules, pigment and nerves.',d:'path',c:'immunodeficiency'},
{q:'A 4-month-old has persistent thrush, chronic diarrhoea and failure to thrive. There is no thymic shadow; T and B cells are absent, but NK cells are present. Most likely defect?',o:['RAG1 or RAG2','IL-2 receptor common γ chain','Bruton tyrosine kinase','Adenosine deaminase'],a:0,x:'RAG enzymes cut and join the gene segments of both T- and B-cell receptors, so both lineages fail while NK cells, which need no rearranged receptor, survive. A common γ chain defect is the tempting choice, but it removes T and NK cells and leaves B cells present.',d:'path',c:'immunodeficiency'}
]};

BOOK['c4-graft']={depth:4,title:'The Stranger\'s Graft',
epi:'Sew a stranger\'s tissue into a body, and the guards will remember its face for as long as it lives.',
c:['hypersensitivity','anemia','transplant','transfusion'],
S:[
{h:'Rejection',p:[
'Transplanted organs are recognised as foreign mainly through **HLA** differences, and recipient T cells meet donor HLA in two ways. In **direct allorecognition**, donor dendritic cells carried inside the graft travel to the recipient\'s lymph nodes, and recipient T cells react to the intact foreign MHC on their surface. A large share of all T cells cross-react with some foreign MHC, so this drives the strong acute cellular rejection of the first months. In **indirect allorecognition**, the recipient\'s own antigen-presenting cells take up shed donor MHC and show its peptides on self MHC class II to CD4 cells. This slower path helps B cells make donor-specific antibodies and keeps chronic rejection going long after the donor\'s cells have gone.',
'Before transplant the team checks ABO compatibility, types HLA (HLA-A, -B and -DR matter most), and performs a **crossmatch**: recipient serum against donor lymphocytes, to find antibodies made after earlier transfusions, pregnancies or transplants. Rejection comes in four tempos.'],
table:{cols:['Type','Timing','Mechanism','Picture'],rows:[
['Hyperacute','minutes','pre-formed antibodies (ABO, HLA) → type II','organ turns mottled and cyanotic on the table; thrombosis'],
['Acute','weeks to months','CD8 T cells against donor MHC (cellular) or new anti-donor antibodies (humoral, C4d deposits)','vasculitis, lymphocytic infiltrate; reversible with immunosuppression'],
['Chronic','months to years','both, through indirect recognition; chronic low-grade injury','fibrosis and intimal thickening: interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy (kidney), bronchiolitis obliterans (lung), vanishing bile ducts (liver), accelerated coronary disease (heart)'],
['Graft-versus-host disease','weeks after bone marrow, stem cell or liver transplant','donor T cells attack the recipient','rash (palms and soles first), cholestatic jaundice, diarrhoea; may also kill leukaemia (graft-versus-tumour)']]},
s:[['phys','Irradiate blood products for immunocompromised recipients to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease.'],['micro','Cytomegalovirus is the most important infection in the first months after transplant; a seronegative recipient of an organ from a seropositive donor is most at risk, so valganciclovir prophylaxis is given. Epstein-Barr virus drives post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder, treated by reducing immunosuppression and with rituximab.']]},
{h:'Immunosuppressants',p:[
'A T cell needs three signals to answer, and each is a drug target. **Signal 1**: the T-cell receptor meets MHC and peptide; calcium rises and **calcineurin** removes a phosphate from NFAT, which enters the nucleus and switches on the IL-2 gene (blocked by ciclosporin and tacrolimus). **Signal 2**: CD28 on the T cell binds B7 (CD80/86) on the antigen-presenting cell (blocked by belatacept). **Signal 3**: IL-2 binds its receptor (CD25 is its α chain) and, through **mTOR**, drives the cell into the cycle (blocked by basiliximab at the receptor and sirolimus at mTOR). Dividing lymphocytes then need new purines, which they make mostly from scratch, so mycophenolate and azathioprine starve them.',
'Most regimens combine a calcineurin inhibitor, an antiproliferative and a steroid. All raise the risk of infection and of virus-driven and skin cancers.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Mechanism','Main toxicity'],rows:[
['Ciclosporin','binds cyclophilin → blocks calcineurin → no IL-2','nephrotoxicity, hypertension, hyperkalaemia, gout, gum hyperplasia, hirsutism'],
['Tacrolimus','binds FKBP → blocks calcineurin','nephrotoxicity, diabetes, neurotoxicity (tremor); no gum hyperplasia or hirsutism'],
['Sirolimus (rapamycin), everolimus','binds FKBP → blocks mTOR → no response to IL-2','hyperlipidaemia, pancytopenia, poor wound healing, mouth ulcers; spares the kidney'],
['Mycophenolate','blocks IMP dehydrogenase → no guanine for lymphocytes','diarrhoea, marrow suppression, teratogen'],
['Azathioprine','6-mercaptopurine, purine antagonist','marrow suppression; dose down with allopurinol; check TPMT'],
['Belatacept','CTLA-4-Ig: binds B7, blocks co-stimulation','post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder in EBV-negative recipients'],
['Basiliximab','anti-IL-2 receptor (CD25)','induction'],
['Antithymocyte globulin','polyclonal antibodies that deplete T cells','induction or steroid-resistant rejection; fever and chills from cytokine release, serum sickness'],
['Glucocorticoids','block NF-κB, cytokine genes','hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, Cushing features']]},
s:[['pharm','Calcineurin inhibitors and many drugs depend on CYP3A4: azoles, macrolides, grapefruit raise their levels; rifampicin, St John\'s wort, carbamazepine lower them.'],['path','Calcineurin inhibitors constrict the afferent arteriole, so kidney function falls with high levels, and years of use scar the kidney. Long-term immunosuppression raises cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (the commonest cancer after transplant), EBV lymphoma and Kaposi sarcoma.']]},
{h:'Antibodies made into drugs',p:[
'Monoclonal antibodies and fusion proteins block one target each, and each target predicts the infection risk. Blocking TNF unseals granulomas, so latent TB can reactivate. Removing B cells lets hepatitis B return. Blocking white cells from entering the brain lets the JC virus grow there.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Target','Used for','Watch for'],rows:[
['Infliximab, adalimumab; etanercept (a receptor decoy)','TNF-α','rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, ankylosing spondylitis, IBD (not etanercept)','latent TB reactivation, fungal infection, demyelination, worse heart failure, drug-induced lupus'],
['Rituximab','CD20 on B cells','B-cell lymphoma, CLL, rheumatoid arthritis, ANCA vasculitis','hepatitis B reactivation, PML, infusion reactions'],
['Tocilizumab','IL-6 receptor','rheumatoid arthritis, giant cell arteritis, cytokine release syndrome','infection, raised lipids, bowel perforation'],
['Ustekinumab','p40 shared by IL-12 and IL-23','psoriasis, Crohn disease','infection'],
['Secukinumab, ixekizumab','IL-17A','psoriasis, spondyloarthritis','Candida infection, IBD flares'],
['Omalizumab','IgE','allergic asthma, chronic urticaria','rare anaphylaxis'],
['Mepolizumab; benralizumab','IL-5; IL-5 receptor','eosinophilic asthma, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis','injection reactions'],
['Dupilumab','IL-4 receptor α (blocks IL-4 and IL-13)','atopic dermatitis, asthma','conjunctivitis'],
['Natalizumab','α4 integrin','multiple sclerosis, Crohn disease','PML (JC virus)'],
['Belimumab','BAFF (B-cell survival factor)','SLE','infection']]},
key:'In the older naming, -ximab is chimeric, -zumab humanised and -umab fully human; -cept marks a receptor fused to an antibody Fc.',
s:[['micro','Before an anti-TNF drug or rituximab, test for latent TB (interferon-γ release assay) and hepatitis B (HBsAg and anti-HBc), and treat or give prophylaxis first.']]},
{h:'Blood groups and transfusion reactions',p:[
'Group O has anti-A and anti-B (IgM) and no antigens: the universal red cell donor. Group AB has no antibodies: the universal plasma donor and universal red cell recipient. These antibodies are "natural": they form in infancy against look-alike sugars on gut bacteria, without any transfusion.',
'**Rh**: an Rh-negative mother exposed to Rh-positive fetal blood makes anti-D IgG, which crosses the placenta in later pregnancies and causes **haemolytic disease of the newborn**, from mild jaundice to fetal anaemia and hydrops. Anti-D immunoglobulin at 28 weeks and within 72 hours after delivery of an Rh-positive baby prevents sensitisation; the Kleihauer-Betke test measures how much fetal blood entered the mother and sets the dose. ABO disease (a group O mother with an A or B baby) is commoner but mild and can occur in a first pregnancy, because group O mothers make some IgG anti-A and anti-B.',
'Products: packed red cells (one unit raises haemoglobin by about 10 g/L in an adult), platelets, fresh frozen plasma (all clotting factors) and cryoprecipitate (fibrinogen, factor VIII, von Willebrand factor, factor XIII).'],
table:{cols:['Reaction','Timing','Mechanism'],rows:[
['Acute haemolytic','within an hour','ABO-incompatible: complement lysis, fever, flank pain, haemoglobinuria, DIC'],
['Febrile non-haemolytic','within hours','cytokines from donor white cells; prevented by leukoreduction'],
['Allergic / anaphylactic','minutes','IgE to donor plasma proteins; anaphylaxis in IgA deficiency'],
['TRALI','within 6 hours','donor anti-leukocyte antibodies activate recipient neutrophils in the lung: non-cardiogenic oedema'],
['TACO','within hours','volume overload: hypertension, raised JVP'],
['Septic','during or soon after','bacteria in the unit, most often platelets, which are stored warm: high fever, rigors, hypotension'],
['Delayed haemolytic','days to weeks','anamnestic antibody to minor antigens (Kidd, Rh, Kell); extravascular haemolysis'],
['Transfusion-associated GVHD','1–6 weeks','donor lymphocytes engraft in an immunocompromised or HLA-similar recipient: rash, diarrhoea, liver injury, marrow failure; prevented by irradiation']]},
s:[['phys','Massive transfusion: citrate in stored blood binds calcium (hypocalcaemia with tingling and arrhythmia), old red cells leak potassium (hyperkalaemia), cold units cause hypothermia, and red cells alone dilute platelets and clotting factors, so plasma and platelets are given in a fixed ratio.'],['micro','People lacking the Duffy antigen (common in West African ancestry) resist Plasmodium vivax, which uses Duffy to enter red cells.']]},
{h:'Vaccines',p:[
'**Live attenuated** vaccines (MMR, varicella, yellow fever, oral polio, rotavirus, intranasal influenza, BCG) give strong, long cellular and humoral immunity but can revert and are contraindicated in pregnancy and severe immunodeficiency. **Inactivated or subunit** vaccines (hepatitis A, injected polio, rabies, influenza injection, hepatitis B, HPV, pertussis) are safe in the immunocompromised but need boosters. **Toxoids** (tetanus, diphtheria) raise antibodies against the toxin. **Conjugate** vaccines link a polysaccharide capsule to a protein so that T cells help, which lets infants under two respond (Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal). **mRNA** vaccines make the host cells produce the antigen.',
'In pregnancy, inactivated vaccines are given to protect both mother and baby through IgG that crosses the placenta: inactivated influenza, Tdap in each pregnancy (from about 27 to 36 weeks, against pertussis in the newborn) and RSV vaccine late in pregnancy. Live vaccines wait until after delivery.'],
s:[['micro','Hepatitis B vaccine is recombinant HBsAg: vaccinated people have anti-HBs only, with no anti-HBc.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A kidney becomes mottled and cyanotic minutes after the clamps are released. Cause?',o:['Donor T cells','Recipient CD8 cells','Chronic vascular injury','Pre-formed recipient antibodies'],a:3,x:'Hyperacute rejection: antibodies already present against ABO or HLA antigens bind the graft endothelium at once, fix complement and clot the vessels. Recipient CD8 cells cause acute cellular rejection, which needs days to weeks to develop.',d:'path',c:'transplant'},
{q:'A liver transplant recipient on tacrolimus starts clarithromycin. Expected effect?',o:['Higher tacrolimus level and nephrotoxicity','Lower tacrolimus level and rejection','No interaction','Loss of the induction effect'],a:0,x:'Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4, which clears tacrolimus, so levels rise and the kidney suffers. A falling level with rejection is what an inducer such as rifampicin would cause.',d:'pharm',c:'transplant'},
{q:'Respiratory distress with bilateral infiltrates 2 hours after a plasma transfusion; JVP and blood pressure are normal. Most likely?',o:['TACO','Anaphylaxis','Acute haemolytic reaction','TRALI'],a:3,x:'TRALI is non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema within 6 hours, caused by donor anti-leukocyte antibodies activating the recipient\'s neutrophils in the lung. TACO also floods the lungs, but from volume overload, so the JVP and blood pressure are raised.',d:'path',c:'transfusion'},
{q:'Which drug blocks mTOR?',o:['Ciclosporin','Tacrolimus','Sirolimus','Mycophenolate'],a:2,x:'Sirolimus binds FKBP, and that complex inhibits mTOR, so T cells cannot respond to IL-2 (signal 3). Tacrolimus binds the same FKBP but its complex blocks calcineurin instead (signal 1).',d:'pharm',c:'transplant'},
{q:'Why can a conjugate vaccine protect infants against Hib when a plain polysaccharide vaccine cannot?',o:['It is a live vaccine','The protein carrier recruits T-cell help','It induces IgE','It activates complement directly'],a:1,x:'Linking the capsule to a protein lets B cells present carrier peptides to helper T cells, giving class switching, memory and a response before age two. Plain polysaccharides are T-independent and give weak, short-lived IgM in infants; conjugate vaccines are not live.',d:'micro',c:'antibodies'},
{q:'Before infliximab is started for Crohn disease, which test is essential?',o:['Interferon-γ release assay for latent TB','HLA-B27','Anti-dsDNA','Serum IgA level'],a:0,x:'TNF keeps granulomas intact, so blocking it can reactivate latent TB, which must be found and treated first (hepatitis B is also checked). Anti-dsDNA can appear during anti-TNF treatment as drug-induced lupus, but it is not a required screen.',d:'pharm',c:'ibd'},
{q:'An Rh(D)-negative woman with no anti-D antibodies delivers an Rh-positive baby. What prevents haemolytic disease in her next pregnancy?',o:['Nothing, because she has not been sensitised','Anti-D immunoglobulin within 72 hours of delivery','Intravenous immunoglobulin for the newborn','Group O blood for any later transfusion'],a:1,x:'Delivery pushes fetal red cells into her blood; anti-D given within 72 hours clears them before she can make her own anti-D, and the Kleihauer-Betke test sets the dose. Waiting because she is not yet sensitised misses the point: sensitisation happens at this delivery.',d:'path',c:'transfusion'}
]};
